      "body": "You would use `on_commit_task()` just like you would use the regular `task()` - although take care because it doesn't behave the same as regular `task()` (it does not provide the same facilities, such as scheduling, etc).\r\n\r\n```python\r\n\r\n@on_commit_task()\r\ndef do_work(obj_id):\r\n    obj = Obj.objects.get(pk=obj_id)\r\n    # Do some work.\r\n\r\n@transaction.atomic\r\ndef some_view(...):\r\n    obj = Obj.objects.create(...)\r\n    do_work(obj.id)  # This job will be enqueued *after* the transaction commits.\r\n    return response\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe main purpose of this decorator is when you need to specifically enqueue a task *after* the active transaction commits, for example in a view. If you don't use `on_commit_task()` there is a possibility of the task being enqueued and executed by the consumer before the transaction has committed (and thus the task will fail because the database row does not exist, e.g.).",
      "author_association": "OWNER",
